---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import ComponentPage from "../../../layouts/ComponentPage.astro";
import ButtonDemo from "../../../components/ButtonDemo";
import InstallCommand from "../../../components/InstallCommand.astro";
import { codeTheme } from "../../../components/code-theme";
import { componentExamples } from "../../../data/snippets";

const { variantsExample, statusExample } = componentExamples.button;
---

<ComponentPage slug="button">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Styled anchors use the same classes through <code>buttonVariants</code>.
  </Fragment>

  <ButtonDemo slot="demo" client:visible />

  <Fragment slot="examples">
    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">Variants and sizes</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      The Preview tab shows every variant and size. Use one <code>default</code> button for the main
      action of a view, <code>outline</code> or <code>ghost</code> for the others, and
      <code>destructive</code> only for actions that delete or cannot be undone.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={variantsExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>

    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">Loading</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      <code>loading</code> shows a spinner and blocks every activation path, including implicit form
      submission. The button keeps its accessible name and stays focusable, so keyboard and screen
      reader users keep their place. It sets <code>aria-busy</code> and <code>aria-disabled</code>,
      but screen readers generally do not announce <code>aria-busy</code> on a button.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      The button shows the pointer cursor. While loading it shows the progress cursor, and a
      disabled button ignores the pointer.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      Announce progress yourself with a status region next to the button. Render it before the
      message changes, and keep it outside the button so the button's name stays stable:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={statusExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <p slot="install-notes" class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    The button uses the standard shadcn tokens and the <code>cn()</code> helper you already have, so
    installing it does not change your theme. It creates
    <code>components/ui/button.tsx</code>, adds <code>class-variance-authority</code> and appends
    the icon <code>motion</code> keyframes (<code>@keyframes buril-icon-*</code>) to your CSS;
    nothing else in your CSS changes.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Coming from shadcn/ui</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    Buril installs to the same path as shadcn's Button, <code>components/ui/button.tsx</code>. If
    you already have that file, the CLI asks before replacing it. The variant names match, but the
    API differs:
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
    <li>
      <strong>
        No <code>asChild</code>.
      </strong>{" "}
      A link stays a link: style an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>
      with <code data-wrap>className=&#123;buttonVariants()&#125;</code> instead of wrapping it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Icon sizes, but no extra-small ones.</strong> <code>size="icon"</code>,
      <code>icon-sm</code> and <code>icon-lg</code> exist (40, 32 and 48px squares).
      <code>xs</code> and <code>icon-xs</code> do not: the smallest size is 32px. For icon-only
      buttons, use <code>IconButton</code>, which requires <code>aria-label</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>
        <code>type="button"</code> by default.
      </strong>{" "}
      shadcn leaves the native default, which submits inside a form. Pass <code>type="submit"</code>{" "}
      for submit buttons.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>
        A <code>loading</code> prop.
      </strong>{" "}
      It blocks activation while keeping focus and the accessible name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>
        No <code>data-slot</code>, <code>data-variant</code> or <code>data-size</code> attributes.
      </strong>
      Update any CSS or tests that select on them.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    Before replacing shadcn's file, search your project for <code>asChild</code>,
    <code>size="xs"</code>, <code>size="icon-xs"</code> and <code>data-slot="button"</code>. Check
    that every <code>size="icon"</code> button has an <code>aria-label</code>. To try Buril next to
    shadcn's Button, answer no when the CLI asks, and copy the source above into a file with another
    name.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Optional theme</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    The Buril palette (ink primary; red lacquer for focus, decoration, the hover wash and
    destructive actions) is a separate install:
  </p>
  <InstallCommand id="install-theme" commands={["add @buril/theme"]} />
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">It edits your global CSS file:</p>
  <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
    <li>
      It replaces your shadcn color tokens in <code>:root</code> and <code>.dark</code>. Other
      variables, such as <code>--radius</code>, stay as they are.
    </li>
    <li>
      It adds two decorative tokens, <code>--decor</code> and <code>--grid</code>, and font tokens.
    </li>
    <li>
      It follows the system without JavaScript: under{" "}
      <code data-wrap>prefers-color-scheme: dark</code>, the dark palette applies unless{" "}
      <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> has the <code>light</code> class.
    </li>
    <li>
      It redefines the <code>dark:</code> variant the same way, so <code>dark:</code> utilities also
      follow the system. A theme toggle must set <code>class="light"</code> on
      <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> to force light mode; removing <code>dark</code> is not enough. With
      next-themes, <code>attribute="class"</code> already does this.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ComponentPage>
